```
# ============================================================================
# Detection: Encoded or Obfuscated PowerShell Execution
# Detection ID: DE-EX-001
# MITRE ATT&CK: T1059.001 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell
# Data Source: Windows PowerShell Operational Log (4104) / Sysmon 1
# Severity: High
# ============================================================================

index=endpoint sourcetype="WinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational" EventCode=4104
| regex ScriptBlockText="(?i)(-enc|-e |frombase64string|invoke-expression|iex|downloadstring|net\.webclient|hidden|bypass|-nop|reflectiveload|invoke-mimikatz)"
| eval obf_indicators=mvcount(split(replace(ScriptBlockText,"[^-]*(-enc|-e |frombase64string|invoke-expression|iex|downloadstring|hidden|bypass)","$1|"),"|"))-1
| stats count min(_time) as first_seen values(ScriptBlockText) as script_snippets by ComputerName, UserID
| eval first_seen=strftime(first_seen,"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
| where count>0
| sort -count
```

## Detection Logic
PowerShell Script Block Logging (4104) captures the deobfuscated script content even when the attacker base64-encodes or obfuscates the command line, making this far more resilient than 4688/Sysmon-1 command-line matching alone.

## False Positive Considerations
Legitimate admin scripts and RMM tooling frequently use -EncodedCommand for whitespace-safe remoting. Baseline your RMM tool's known script hashes and exclude.

## Tuning Notes
Enable Script Block Logging + Module Logging org-wide via GPO; this detection is materially weaker without it.
